<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Find Intercepts: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02073</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Find Intercepts: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>587b7ed7-e2c3-40dd-8412-dc5c53fa7741</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <note class="mini-lesson-question" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">What are the \(x\)-intercept(s) of the graph of \(y=(2x-9)(x+2)\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Find the \(x\)-Intercepts</title>
      <para id="para-00002">The \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of an equation occur when \(y = 0\).</para>
      <para id="para-00003"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00004">For the equation \(y=(x-9)(x+2)\), look at the factors \((x-9)\) and \((x+2)\). Notice in the first factor, \((x-9)\), \(x=9\) makes the factor equal to 0. Substitute 9 for \(x\) in the equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">\(y=(9-9)(9+2)\) \(y=(0)(11)\) \(y=0\)</para>
      <para id="para-00006">Notice in the second factor, \((x+2)\), \(x=-2\) makes the factor equal to 0. Substitute -2 for \(x\) in the equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00007">\(y=(-2-9)(-2+2)\) \(y=(-11)(0)\) \(y=0\)</para>
      <para id="para-00008">In the equation \(y=(x-9)(x+2)\), \(y=0\) when \(x=9\) and \(x=-2\). The \(x\)-intercepts occur when \(x=9\) and \(x=-2\). To find the \(x\)-intercepts of a quadratic equation in factored form, set each factor equal to zero and solve for \(x\).</para>
      <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00010">Find the \(x\)-intercepts for \(y=(2x+7)(x-4)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00011">The \(x\)-intercepts occur when \(y = 0\). Set each factor to 0 and solve.</para>
      <para id="para-00012">\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+7&amp;=&amp;0\;\;\;\;\;x-4&amp;=&amp;0\\x&amp;=&amp;-3.5\;\;\;\;\;\;x&amp;=&amp;4\end{array}\)</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Find the \(x\)-Intercepts</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00013">Find the \(x\)-intercepts for \(y=(2x-5)(x+3)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00015">Here is how to find the \(x\)-intercepts:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The \(x\)-intercepts occur when \(y = 0\). Set each factor to 0 and solve.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x-5&amp;=&amp;0\;\;\;\;\;x+3&amp;=&amp;0\\x&amp;=&amp;2.5\;\;\;\;\;\;x&amp;=&amp;-3\end{array}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="c70e3466-ddbb-46c7-8eb3-670add88a2bd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00018">What are the \(x\)-intercept(s) of the graph of \(y=(2x+1)(x-3)\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00019">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00020">\(x = -3\), \(x = 0.5\)</para>
            <para id="para-00021">\(x = 3\), \(x = -0.5\)</para>
            <para id="para-00022">\(x = -3\), \(x = 1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00023">\(x = 3\), \(x = -1\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Take a moment to think about what you learned in the mini-lesson review.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
